                <pb facs="0740" n="630" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg630"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Ryan to Mr. Blaine.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-03-05"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 5, 1890</date>.
                        (Received March 17.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 255.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In connection with your No. 206 of February
                    20, 1890, in regard to the case of Capt. J. H. Stilphen, of the American
                    schooner <hi rendition="#italic">Robert Ruff</hi>, I beg to forward, for your
                    consideration, notes from the foreign office, with translations, relative
                    thereto; also copy of a communication from Mr. J. D. Hoff, our consul at Vera
                    Cruz, stating that the case of Captain Stilphen had been removed to Vera Cruz,
                    for reexamination, to the district court.</p>
                <p>You will observe that the Mexican Government insists that Captain Stilphen was
                    within its jurisdiction (2½ miles from shore) when he aided the escape of Joseph
                    Patton.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thos. Ryan.</hi></signed></closer>





